It's just a simple demo app and it's really there so I can showcase some of the tools and like things that I'm thinking about for the video.

32
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:57,892
So let's start off with the obvious.

33
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:06,560
I think we can all safely say with the advent of AI coding, the amount of code that people are generating is going through the roof.

34
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:09,040
It's like so much more code.

35
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:18,480
People are writing a lot more features, they're writing more tests, they're just doing a lot more because one person can spin up multiple agents and generate a ton of code.

36
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:28,400
And to be honest, it's almost impossible of a task for a single engineer to review even their own code, let alone do code reviews for other people.

37
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:31,972
So, where do I stand right now personally for code reviews?

38
00:02:32,494 --> 00:02:34,880
And the answer is it depends.

39
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:44,292
I think like any good answer to any hard problem, answer is really it depends because the reality of code reviews is a gradient.

40
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:47,040
It really depends on the code that you're reviewing.

41
00:02:47,214 --> 00:02:59,040
So when you're writing code and when you're building a feature, the AI agents should be part of the entire code's journey to production, uh, from planning, building, reviewing, and launching.

42
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:04,960
Every single part of this, you should actually include your AI agents throughout the whole process, right?

43
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:15,280
Whether it's planning out a spec to build out, um, making sure things are feature complete and making sure you have like evaluations and like validation criteria that you're thinking about ahead of time.

44
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:25,120
And when you're building the code, you want to have agentic validation so that the AI can self-validate and self-fix itself and then get the code right a lot more often.

45
00:03:25,390 --> 00:03:28,772
And then we have the review, which is like what we're talking about today.

46
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:32,320
And we're going to talk about some strategies on how you should do this.

47
00:03:32,558 --> 00:03:36,160
And then when you're launching, I think this also should be part of it.

48
00:03:36,334 --> 00:03:38,980
You want to feature gate and run experiments on your code.

49
00:03:39,374 --> 00:03:46,480
And all of these things I think tie together to answer the question when and how much should you review a code?

50
00:03:46,734 --> 00:03:47,920
So it really depends, right?

51
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:54,080
Now, the big concept that I want to let you guys know about today is that I think of the code base like a tree.

52
00:03:54,848 --> 00:03:59,408
And like a tree, there are different parts of the tree that is more important.

53
00:03:59,648 --> 00:04:05,008
So think of like kind of the trunk, the root, the main part of the code.

54
00:04:05,168 --> 00:04:11,328
That's like your main entry points, like your app.js that has all of the different helpers that are like added to it.

55
00:04:11,566 --> 00:04:20,368
And then your infrastructure code, like image processing, networking, and all these things that if something goes wrong in that code base, it can affect the entire app.

56
00:04:20,608 --> 00:04:22,528
I think of that as like a trunk piece of code.

57
00:04:22,638 --> 00:04:23,844
And then there's like leaf notes.

58
00:04:24,048 --> 00:04:26,768
And we're going to get into more of this topic throughout this video.

59
00:04:26,928 --> 00:04:29,568
But start thinking about your code base as a trunk.

60
00:04:29,728 --> 00:04:45,008
And as soon as you start thinking about this, then the obvious next thing that you naturally think about is that the review depth, the depth that your own effort level on reviewing a certain code, it should really be based on like the blast radius of that code change.

61
00:04:45,102 --> 00:04:56,580
As an example, if you're touching something that's like core image rendering infrastructure that touches all the different image renderings, and there's a lot of downstream dependencies, then I would spend a lot of time reading that piece of code, for example.

62
00:04:57,328 --> 00:05:10,532
and then for any like one off leaf node code, you could probably just have agents do simple validations like component testing or snapshot testing and just visually validate the PR as long as they have proof in the PR.

63
00:05:10,768 --> 00:05:17,888
So this concept of like changing your effort level depending on the importance of the code, I think really matters a lot.

64
00:05:18,048 --> 00:05:20,288
And you should actually do this planning from the beginning.

65
00:05:20,462 --> 00:05:24,688
So when you're planning the entire feature, you actually want to feature gate it.

66
00:05:24,768 --> 00:05:33,988
Now, if you don't know what feature gating is, is essentially adding a branching logic in your code base where you can say, hey, this feature is on or off.

67
00:05:34,286 --> 00:05:40,848
So there's a concept called feature toggling, a really popular option, it's called launch starkly, and you could have feature gating.

68
00:05:40,928 --> 00:05:49,248
There's a lot of actually infrastructure that you need to do feature gating properly, but you can start out really simple by just adding a Boolean flag in your code base.

69
00:05:49,328 --> 00:05:52,228
It's a gating code and you just turn things on and off.

70
00:05:52,448 --> 00:06:01,156
So from the beginning, if you're planning a new feature, think in broad strokes and gate all of the things out, and then think a lot about the integration pieces.

71
00:06:01,454 --> 00:06:09,168
So you want to separate out leaf nodes, things that don't touch any of the existing code bases, and then you want to think about integration separately.

72
00:06:09,328 --> 00:06:11,588
You really want to like gate those integration layers.

73
00:06:11,822 --> 00:06:19,248
So once you have like a good solid plan and a good like roadmap and you have like good feature gating like ready to go, then you can start coding.

74
00:06:19,328 --> 00:06:27,328
And then this coding part is like, you know, just use agents to build the code out, and then you can just use the agents to like you know make atomic changes.

75
00:06:27,488 --> 00:06:33,060
I have a ton of videos around using agents to code, but you know, we're not covering that particular topic.

76
00:06:33,248 --> 00:06:37,248
But once you're ready for a PR, what should go in that PR?

77
00:06:37,422 --> 00:06:38,208
So let's think about that.

78
00:06:38,368 --> 00:06:47,076
So, in my opinion, for your diffs to be really like solid, your PRs to be like rock solid, you really want to anchor and push your agents to give you proof.

79
00:06:47,168 --> 00:06:50,340
And there's many different types of proof that the agents could give you.

80
00:06:50,528 --> 00:06:52,644
There can be just straight up unit tests.

81
00:06:52,878 --> 00:06:56,420
So, as an example, something like this it's just a straight up unit test.

82
00:06:56,768 --> 00:06:58,532
Very simple, a lot of tests.

83
00:06:58,958 --> 00:07:03,844
And this is like just reading the test names and just skimming the tests and the asserts.

84
00:07:03,968 --> 00:07:08,768
You could quickly get some like good sense of, oh, there's like good logic testing that's happening.

85
00:07:08,928 --> 00:07:12,676
One side note is you want to be careful here when the agents are writing tests.

86
00:07:12,848 --> 00:07:17,168
You should probably make your own skill around not writing useless tests.

87
00:07:17,408 --> 00:07:18,608
That's like a little side tip.

88
00:07:18,848 --> 00:07:20,868
And then you also want runtime evidence.

89
00:07:21,038 --> 00:07:32,356
So let's say that you have a lot of like runtime logs, and I talk about this in my angel tech engineering video where you want to really anchor on giving more visibility and more eyes for the agents.

90
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:38,208
So you can have runtime evidence, and then you could have visual evidence where maybe it's a screenshot.

91
00:07:38,368 --> 00:07:45,888
And then I also ask the agents to give a confidence level on their code and their gating and like different parts of the code.

92
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:50,848
So you really want to push your agents when you're ready to like push out a PR on these kinds of things.

93
00:07:51,168 --> 00:07:52,128
Like, do you have evidence?

94
00:07:52,288 --> 00:07:54,116
Obviously, you want to build these into your skills.

95
00:07:54,368 --> 00:07:57,168
So let's take a look at like UI proof, right?

96
00:07:57,294 --> 00:08:06,128
So, one of the things I like to do every time I make changes with the AI, I always ask it to have a video or a UI screenshot, right?

97
00:08:06,222 --> 00:08:10,048
So for this one, this lets the player save a card, right?

98
00:08:10,318 --> 00:08:11,488
So you can see right here.

99
00:08:12,128 --> 00:08:13,488
There is a save button.

100
00:08:13,568 --> 00:08:15,332
So I can see that Oh, okay, this was saved.

101
00:08:15,488 --> 00:08:17,316
And here's some like simple code changes.

102
00:08:17,488 --> 00:08:19,408
And then it looks roughly right.

103
00:08:19,488 --> 00:08:21,488
But the point is, I see a visual proof.

104
00:08:21,568 --> 00:08:26,448
So immediately I'm a lot more believing of this AI's code output, right?

105
00:08:26,734 --> 00:08:31,652
So if you have the visual proof and if you know that it's safe, then it's a lot safe to land.

106
00:08:31,888 --> 00:08:38,308
So having proof and having more proof lets you move faster because you don't have to manually check these proofs.

107
00:08:38,574 --> 00:08:41,008
And this is really around agentic validation.

108
00:08:41,198 --> 00:08:44,048
And I talk about that in my agentic engineering video.

109
00:08:44,142 --> 00:08:55,328
But agentic validation is a core concept of being able to read less code because if you have a lot of these proof upfront on the PR, then you don't have to read as much code.

110
00:08:55,488 --> 00:09:00,128
Now, the next big concept, and this is kind of in the tree concept, is leaf code.

111
00:09:00,288 --> 00:09:06,848
A leaf code is really code that is written in isolation or it's completely gated off in a feature gate.

112
00:09:06,928 --> 00:09:14,768
So this can mean like one off components that you can just validate with like a component test or a snapshot test, right?

113
00:09:14,928 --> 00:09:24,580
Or maybe it's new infrastructure, new logic, maybe a new endpoint that you're integrating with, and you could just validate that with you know unit tests or integration tests.

114
00:09:24,688 --> 00:09:29,328
And as long as they're not integrated to the main production code base, you can move really quickly.

115
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:32,608
And then I'll cover later what you should be doing.

116
00:09:32,846 --> 00:09:37,008
So for me, when I'm reviewing leaf like code, I spend a lot less time.

117
00:09:37,088 --> 00:09:44,608
I'm almost just mainly skimming the code and making sure things are not like very off or like something is just obviously wrong.

118
00:09:44,768 --> 00:09:49,808
And for example, what can be a trunk is like you know, in the reducer reducer has a lot of shared state.

119
00:09:49,888 --> 00:09:54,372
So let's say you change some behavior here in this like game action advanced cards.

120
00:09:54,528 --> 00:09:59,248
Maybe there's like a new card type or something like that, and you're changing existing behavior.

121
00:09:59,342 --> 00:10:05,088
And if that behavior is not gated out, then I would spend a lot more time reviewing it because that changes production, right?

122
00:10:05,168 --> 00:10:10,928
And then this has trickle-down effects and side effects, and all these things can really matter and affect production code, right?

123
00:10:11,054 --> 00:10:12,548
So you're really anchoring on safety.

124
00:10:12,846 --> 00:10:21,764
So we kind of covered like thinking about code as like this gradient and how you should anchor on changing your effort level, right?

125
00:10:22,128 --> 00:10:25,808
And in my opinion, everything should be anchored on safety.

126
00:10:25,968 --> 00:10:28,740
What is the control experience that should not change?

127
00:10:28,974 --> 00:10:32,448
And if this change went wrong, how bad can it be?

128
00:10:32,654 --> 00:10:35,168
And then can I roll this change back?

129
00:10:35,310 --> 00:10:37,764
And then that really just depends on feature gating, right?

130
00:10:38,126 --> 00:10:45,568
So if you can answer these questions, especially the last one, then you can spend a lot less time reviewing each individual code.

131
00:10:45,728 --> 00:10:56,208
Now, one other thing that is like not that important in my opinion these days, that is part of like usually reviews is like knits and like little like different ways of writing.

132
00:10:56,368 --> 00:11:00,484
And I would just leverage like static checking here to be able to do a lot of that work.

133
00:11:00,782 --> 00:11:04,516
So, like type checking, linting, and all those kind of things.

134
00:11:04,878 --> 00:11:07,168
And agents are really good at doing stuff like this.

135
00:11:07,310 --> 00:11:17,328
So we have a codex reviewer here, and it's like seeing some linting issues, so it's like saying we should fix it as an example, and here's some like stylistic changes, right?

136
00:11:17,568 --> 00:11:25,808
So, one big thing about code review is that I think the old days of like having your knits and then like, oh, the code should look this way.

137
00:11:25,968 --> 00:11:28,196
This is my favorite way of writing code.

138
00:11:28,368 --> 00:11:29,568
I think that's kind of dead.

139
00:11:29,728 --> 00:11:41,316
I think we write so much code, and code is being a lot more fluid, and refactors are happening a lot more often, and it's a lot easier to do, especially once like there's good validations for the code that you want to refactor.

140
00:11:41,728 --> 00:11:46,608
I think these kind of knits don't really belong in the conversation of code reviews anymore.

141
00:11:46,768 --> 00:11:50,928
So, going back to the original question is do I read the code?

142
00:11:51,150 --> 00:11:53,728
The simple answer is yes, I still read a lot of code.

143
00:11:53,934 --> 00:11:56,740
I think I read more code than I did before.

144
00:11:57,248 --> 00:12:04,768
But I definitely have this like knob where I I first ask myself, is this a core change, like a trunk change?

145
00:12:04,928 --> 00:12:06,608
And is it touch, It's like an infra?

146
00:12:06,848 --> 00:12:08,688
Does it have a lot of like dependencies?

147
00:12:08,848 --> 00:12:10,208
If so, I increase it.

148
00:12:10,368 --> 00:12:11,808
And then I say, is this safe?

149
00:12:11,968 --> 00:12:12,768
Is there gating?

150
00:12:13,198 --> 00:12:14,768
If something went wrong, like what can happen?

151
00:12:14,848 --> 00:12:18,244
So all of these things really change how much I review the code, right?

152
00:12:18,542 --> 00:12:21,488
And then the more dangerous it is, the more I spend time.

153
00:12:21,728 --> 00:12:25,328
More of that changes a one-way door, I spend more time reviewing, right?

154
00:12:25,488 --> 00:12:31,556
Now, let's get kind of to the fun parts of like looking at how you can use agents to kind of help you review your code.

155
00:12:31,694 --> 00:12:35,968
Now, in my opinion, AI is really good at reviewing code already.

156
00:12:36,174 --> 00:12:38,628
I actually do think it's better than most humans.

157
00:12:39,118 --> 00:12:41,968
The issue is a lot of people are doing it wrong.

158
00:12:42,128 --> 00:12:45,968
And in my opinion, there's a few things that you should really anchor on doing.

159
00:12:46,128 --> 00:12:48,208
One, you should have a really good review skill.

160
00:12:48,368 --> 00:12:52,420
I think both clock code and codex have really good built reviews.

161
00:12:52,974 --> 00:12:55,172
And then here is the review agent right here.

162
00:12:55,534 --> 00:12:59,684
And then for clock code, it's slash code review.

163
00:13:00,622 --> 00:13:03,936
So these are all both really good review agents, in my opinion.

164
00:13:04,174 --> 00:13:15,908
But one thing that's kind of important to do is to use a new subagent or a different agent, an agent that doesn't have the context of the code that it was used to create the code because the agents will cheat.

165
00:13:16,238 --> 00:13:18,788
You know, all the context that you gave it, it will cheat.

166
00:13:19,456 --> 00:13:23,204
and then and it will really try to anchor on like a lot of the previous conversation.

167
00:13:23,376 --> 00:13:32,096
So you really want to review the code with like a different sub agent or like a separate agent that is called an adversarial review agent.

168
00:13:32,256 --> 00:13:37,776
And when you have an agent write a diff or thing, just have like a good template that the agent can pull from.

169
00:13:37,998 --> 00:13:42,564
So your PRs look the same, they feel the same, they have the important bits.

170
00:13:42,926 --> 00:13:49,936
If you don't give any guidance on the what the agent should really have, it will write you like a giant paragraph, like a story.

171
00:13:50,126 --> 00:14:00,484
And honestly, that's my biggest pet peeve right now is when people submit PRs or diffs to review where the summary and test plan is longer than the code changes itself.

172
00:14:00,718 --> 00:14:01,536
So don't be that guy.

173
00:14:01,696 --> 00:14:09,284
Now I showed this off a little bit, but all the agent stuff that I've been talking about was during runtime, during the generation of the code, and that's what you're running.

174
00:14:09,614 --> 00:14:21,696
But then Codex actually offers this integration right here where you can add a codex reviewer onto GitHub and it could run on new GitHub, like you know, PR opens, or you could also like just add codex here like that.

175
00:14:21,776 --> 00:14:24,336
And essentially it's this chat GPD Codex connector.

176
00:14:24,416 --> 00:14:28,816
And I think there's a separate model slightly that is custom trained just for like reviewing.

177
00:14:28,976 --> 00:14:30,628
Now, all of this costs money, right?

178
00:14:31,216 --> 00:14:32,656
But yeah, that's an example.

179
00:14:32,816 --> 00:14:36,976
Another thing you can do is actually use goals as an example.

180
00:14:37,326 --> 00:14:50,532
So one of the common issues that people will run into when they start having these agentic systems that are reviewing their code on GitHub is that after you submit a PR, the agent will go do stuff and then it'll give you a bunch of comments that you need to fix.

181
00:14:50,990 --> 00:14:52,896
And sometimes there'll be a lot of back and forth.

182
00:14:52,976 --> 00:14:56,656
You fix it and then like you resubmit, and then it'll be like back and forth.

183
00:14:56,846 --> 00:15:00,816
So a thing you can do is like just use goals to babysit a PR.

184
00:15:00,910 --> 00:15:03,136
So let's say we had this like random PR.

185
00:15:03,216 --> 00:15:08,656
This is a draft, but you could just say, for this PR, can you babysit it every hour?

186
00:15:08,846 --> 00:15:15,456
Check it every hour and make sure that you address any changes requests from the codex agent.

187
00:15:15,536 --> 00:15:17,136
It misspelled it, but you get the point.

188
00:15:17,262 --> 00:15:27,588
So, what this will do is every hour it will check that PR and then it will fix any issues, rerun your local validations, agentic validations, update the PR notes and then resend it.

189
00:15:27,936 --> 00:15:32,816
So these are the kind of things that you should build into your own skill if you haven't done so.

190
00:15:33,038 --> 00:15:37,616
But yeah, so those are kind of like the high-level, like useful tools, in my opinion.

191
00:15:37,776 --> 00:15:41,604
It's just really important to use adversarial agent, in my opinion.

192
00:15:41,806 --> 00:15:43,216
Have good review skills.

193
00:15:43,342 --> 00:15:46,244
I actually just like the default like skill reviewers.

194
00:15:46,576 --> 00:15:56,336
Have a template for your PRs, and you could use my template if you want, but just have a template so it conforms your PRs to that template and then it adds like validation proof.

195
00:15:56,416 --> 00:15:58,656
You know, we talked about the different types of validation.

196
00:15:58,958 --> 00:16:01,924
You really want to anchor on the agents giving you proof.

